# Step 1: Add a feature combining compactness and circularity
# Feature name: COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO
# Feature description: Ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY (COMPACTNESS / CIRCULARITY).
# Usefulness: This feature examines the relationship between how compact a silhouette is and its circular attributes. Silhouettes of different vehicle types might have distinct compactness-to-circularity relationships. For instance, a BUS (rectangular) may have a different ratio compared to a SAAB (more circular features).
df['COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['CIRCULARITY']
end

python
# Step 2: Create a feature measuring variance distribution
# Feature name: VARIANCE_RATIO_MAJOR_MINOR
# Feature description: Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS (SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS / SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS).
# Usefulness: This highlights the relative distribution of variance along the major and minor axes of the silhouette. Vehicles with elongated shapes (e.g., VAN or BUS) may have higher proportions of variance along the major axis, while more compact ones (e.g., SAAB or OPEL) could have lower ratios.
df['VARIANCE_RATIO_MAJOR_MINOR'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']
end

python
# Step 3: Transform skewness along axes to capture non-linearity
# Feature name: SKEWNESS_PRODUCT
# Feature description: Multiply SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS and SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS to represent combined asymmetry across both axes (SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS * SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS).
# Usefulness: Vehicles of different types might have varying levels of asymmetry around their major and minor axes. This interaction helps capture the complexity in silhouette shapes that may not be captured when the individual skewness values are treated separately.
df['SKEWNESS_PRODUCT'] = df['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS'] * df['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS']
end

